# Embedded Sensor-Processing Pipeline for Anomaly Detection

Below is an initial implementation of an embedded sensor-processing pipeline that detects anomalies from accelerometer data. This implementation is designed for resource-constrained embedded systems but can be adapted for more powerful devices.

## System Architecture

```
[Accelerometer] → [Data Collection] → [Preprocessing] → [Feature Extraction] → [Anomaly Detection] → [Alert/Logging]
```

## Implementation (C/C++ for Embedded Systems)

```c
#include <stdint.h>
#include <math.h>
#include <string.h>

// Configuration parameters
#define WINDOW_SIZE 32          // Number of samples in sliding window
#define FEATURE_DIMENSIONS 3    // x, y, z axes
#define THRESHOLD_MULTIPLIER 3.0f // For simple statistical anomaly detection

// Data structures
typedef struct {
    float x;
    float y;
    float z;
} AccelData;

typedef struct {
    float mean[FEATURE_DIMENSIONS];
    float stddev[FEATURE_DIMENSIONS];
} FeatureStats;

// Circular buffer for sliding window
typedef struct {
    AccelData data[WINDOW_SIZE];
    uint8_t head;
    uint8_t count;
} SlidingWindow;

// Global state
static SlidingWindow window;
static FeatureStats baseline_stats;

// Initialize the system
void init_anomaly_detection() {
    memset(&window, 0, sizeof(window));
    memset(&baseline_stats, 0, sizeof(baseline_stats));
}

// Add new accelerometer reading to the window
void add_accel_data(float x, float y, float z) {
    // Store new data
    window.data[window.head].x = x;
    window.data[window.head].y = y;
    window.data[window.head].z = z;

    // Update head pointer and count
    window.head = (window.head + 1) % WINDOW_SIZE;
    if (window.count < WINDOW_SIZE) {
        window.count++;
    }
}

// Calculate basic statistics for the current window
void calculate_window_stats(FeatureStats* stats) {
    if (window.count < WINDOW_SIZE) {
        // Not enough data yet
        return;
    }

    // Calculate mean
    for (int i = 0; i < FEATURE_DIMENSIONS; i++) {
        stats->mean[i] = 0.0f;
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < WINDOW_SIZE; i++) {
        stats->mean[0] += window.data[i].x;
        stats->mean[1] += window.data[i].y;
        stats->mean[2] += window.data[i].z;
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < FEATURE_DIMENSIONS; i++) {
        stats->mean[i] /= WINDOW_SIZE;
    }

    // Calculate standard deviation
    for (int i = 0; i < FEATURE_DIMENSIONS; i++) {
        stats->stddev[i] = 0.0f;
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < WINDOW_SIZE; i++) {
        stats->stddev[0] += powf(window.data[i].x - stats->mean[0], 2);
        stats->stddev[1] += powf(window.data[i].y - stats->mean[1], 2);
        stats->stddev[2] += powf(window.data[i].z - stats->mean[2], 2);
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < FEATURE_DIMENSIONS; i++) {
        stats->stddev[i] = sqrtf(stats->stddev[i] / WINDOW_SIZE);
    }
}

// Update baseline statistics (could be called periodically)
void update_baseline_stats() {
    calculate_window_stats(&baseline_stats);
}

// Simple anomaly detection based on statistical thresholds
int detect_anomaly(float x, float y, float z) {
    if (window.count < WINDOW_SIZE) {
        return 0; // Not enough data
    }

    // Check if current values exceed threshold
    for (int i = 0; i < FEATURE_DIMENSIONS; i++) {
        float value = (i == 0) ? x : (i == 1) ? y : z;
        float threshold = baseline_stats.mean[i] + THRESHOLD_MULTIPLIER * baseline_stats.stddev[i];

        if (fabsf(value) > threshold) {
            return 1; // Anomaly detected
        }
    }

    return 0; // No anomaly
}

// Main processing loop
void process_accel_data(float x, float y, float z) {
    // Add new data to window
    add_accel_data(x, y, z);

    // Check for anomalies
    if (detect_anomaly(x, y, z)) {
        // Handle anomaly (e.g., trigger alert, log event)
        // In embedded systems, this might involve:
        // - Setting a flag
        // - Triggering an interrupt
        // - Writing to a status register
        // - Sending a message to another component
    }

    // Periodically update baseline stats (e.g., every 100 samples)
    static uint16_t counter = 0;
    if (++counter >= 100) {
        update_baseline_stats();
        counter = 0;
    }
}
```

## Key Features

1. **Sliding Window Processing**: Maintains a window of recent accelerometer readings for context-aware analysis.

2. **Basic Statistical Analysis**: Calculates mean and standard deviation for each axis to establish a baseline.

3. **Simple Anomaly Detection**: Flags readings that exceed a threshold based on historical statistics.

4. **Memory Efficiency**: Uses a circular buffer to minimize memory usage.

5. **Embedded-Friendly**: Designed with minimal memory allocation and computational requirements.

