# Minimal Enterprise-Grade Backend Microservice

Below is a minimal working version of a backend microservice designed for a large enterprise with strict type safety requirements and integration capabilities. This example uses TypeScript, Express, and follows enterprise patterns.

## Core Service Structure

```typescript
// src/index.ts - Main service entry point
import express from 'express';
import { createServer } from 'http';
import { config } from './config';
import { logger } from './utils/logger';
import { errorHandler } from './middleware/errorHandler';
import { requestLogger } from './middleware/requestLogger';
import { healthRoutes } from './routes/health';
import { apiRoutes } from './routes/api';
import { connectToDatabase } from './database';
import { initializeCorporateIntegrations } from './integrations';

async function bootstrap() {
  try {
    // Initialize database connection
    await connectToDatabase();

    // Initialize corporate system integrations
    await initializeCorporateIntegrations();

    // Create Express app
    const app = express();

    // Middleware
    app.use(express.json());
    app.use(requestLogger);

    // Routes
    app.use('/health', healthRoutes);
    app.use('/api', apiRoutes);

    // Error handling
    app.use(errorHandler);

    // Create HTTP server
    const server = createServer(app);

    // Start server
    server.listen(config.port, () => {
      logger.info(`Service running on port ${config.port}`);
    });

    // Graceful shutdown
    process.on('SIGTERM', () => {
      logger.info('SIGTERM received. Shutting down gracefully...');
      server.close(() => {
        logger.info('Server closed');
        process.exit(0);
      });
    });

  } catch (error) {
    logger.error('Service startup failed:', error);
    process.exit(1);
  }
}

bootstrap();
```

